---
import MethodSection from "./MethodSection.astro";

// The private holdout is explained once, in PrivateHoldoutSection (split
// sizes, hashing, limits, flag threshold); defence 1 summarises it and links
// there.
const defences: { title: string; detail: string; link?: { href: string; text: string } }[] = [
  {
    title: "Private holdout",
    detail:
      "A share of every holdout-enabled dataset (20% to 40%, by dataset size) has its human answers withheld from everything we publish. A submitter who copies the published distributions has no signal for the private questions, so their public and private SPS diverge and the row is flagged for review. The split, why it is deterministic, its limits and the flag threshold are described under",
    link: { href: "#private-holdout", text: "Private holdout split" },
  },
  {
    title: "Tiered statistical validation",
    detail:
      "Every submission goes through three tiers of checks. Tier 1 verifies schema, bounds, and distribution validity. Tier 2 recomputes JSD, Kendall's τ, and composite parity from the per-question distributions and rejects any submission whose reported aggregates do not match its per-question data. Tier 3 runs statistical anomaly detectors that compare the submission against the distribution of legitimate runs. They flag implausibly perfect per-question JSD, distributions suspiciously close to the public human data, and deviation from peers in the same model family.",
  },
  {
    title: "Adversarial regression suite",
    detail:
      "Our test harness keeps a set of fabricated submissions that must fail validation: pure answer-key copies, public-copy-with-fabricated-private attacks, constant-offset noise attacks, aggregate inflation, and claimed-model swaps. New detectors are tested against this suite before merge, and any change to existing detector thresholds must keep the suite passing. The suite is versioned with the validator and runs as a CI gate.",
  },
];
---

<MethodSection id="anti-gaming" title="Anti-gaming defences" fold>
  <p class="sb-muted sb-measure" slot="lede">
    Our threat model and defences draw on recent adversarial analyses of AI benchmarks, in particular Wang
    et al. (UC Berkeley, 2026) [<a href="#anti-gaming-ref-1">1</a>]. They pushed eight widely used agent
    benchmarks to near-perfect scores without solving any of the tasks.
  </p>

  <p class="sb-prose">
    SynthBench is a submission-artifact benchmark: participants submit a JSON file and we score it. Attacks
    that assume an evaluating agent running alongside the submission (sandbox escapes,
    <code class="sb-mcode">eval()</code> on agent input, binary-trojan wrappers, LLM-judge prompt injection)
    do not apply. The general pattern behind Wang et al.'s attacks, targeting the evaluator instead of the
    task, does apply. We have three lines of defence against it.
  </p>

  <ol class="sb-msteps">
    {
      defences.map((d) => (
        <li>
          <h3 class="sb-h3">{d.title}</h3>
          <p>
            {d.detail}
            {d.link && (
              <>
                {" "}
                <a href={d.link.href}>{d.link.text}</a>.
              </>
            )}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <h3 class="sb-h3 sb-mh3">Null-agent baselines</h3>
  <div class="sb-prose">
    <p>
      Following Wang et al.'s recommendation, we also track two "null agent" baselines over time: a
      uniform-random baseline and a majority-class baseline. A model has to score above these floors for its
      result to be non-trivial. On a stable dataset, an upward drift in baseline SPS would indicate a bug in
      the scoring function. CI monitors for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Their current scores per dataset are in the <a href="#anchors">reference points table</a> above; random
      answers are the Index's 0. CI enforces <code class="sb-mcode">random-baseline SPS &lt; 0.80</code> and
      <code class="sb-mcode">majority-baseline SPS &lt; 0.85</code>. The Berkeley paper's target for the
      random baseline is 0.70. The remaining gap comes from SynthBench's
      <code class="sb-mcode">p_refuse</code> term, which rewards agreement with human DK/Refused rates. The
      git log of <code class="sb-mcode">docs/baseline-floors-log.md</code> is the canonical drift record.
    </p>
    <p>
      We err on the conservative side. For a scientific leaderboard, it is better to hold a legitimate
      superhuman submission for human review than to accept a fabricated one. Submitters whose runs are
      flagged can appeal. At validation time, the null hypothesis is that suspicious-looking data was
      fabricated.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="sb-msub sb-mt" id="anti-gaming-ref-1">
    <div class="sb-kicker">Reference [1]</div>
    <p>
      Wang, Y., Mang, K., Cheung, T., Sen, S., &amp; Song, D. (2026).
      <em>How We Broke Top AI Agent Benchmarks</em>. UC Berkeley RDI.{" "}
      <a href="https://rdi.berkeley.edu/blog/trustworthy-benchmarks-cont/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        rdi.berkeley.edu/blog/trustworthy-benchmarks-cont<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</MethodSection>
